diff --git a/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md b/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
index 46b3f8e..bbf9517 100644
--- a/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
+++ b/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@ ## Shared construction and simulation
 
 - `scenes/world.tscn` and `scripts/world_manager.gd` provide the shared construction world. Sandbox, campaign, puzzle editing and Test as Player use the same BuildManager and SimulationManager.
 - BuildManager owns placement, connector snapping, selection, configuration and snapshot history. Validate complete proposed edits before committing. Group edits are atomic; one forbidden member rejects the whole operation.
+- SnapManager owns one candidate/validation path: exposed compatible track connectors, cursor-directed physical surface hits for scenery, then grid fallback. Surface hits select the target; measured occupied volumes and world-space edges decide rigid attachment without changing rotation. Edge-adjacent faces are alternatives, Tab chooses deliberately, and short hysteresis stabilizes the default. Two-pixel ray sampling handles exact silhouette misses. BuildManager preserves the actual grab-plane height and offset for direct dragging. Both preview and commit validate the complete group.
 - Construction history uses Godot's `UndoRedo` with complete layout snapshots. BuildManager registers validated edits without replaying them; undo/redo restores selection by stable IDs. UI and mode resets use the engine's history API.
 - `BuildingPiece` is the common placed-object node for tracks and scenery. PieceDefinition resources own catalog metadata, connectors, bounds and thumbnails. Changing an adjustable piece must update its geometry, collision, connectors and placement bounds together. Reject conflicting changes without leaving partial state.
 - PieceDefinition also owns `normalized_configuration` and `configured`: ordinary definitions retain their geometry; ElevatorDefinition and SupportDefinition derive instance geometry without changing shared resources. BuildingPiece, save loading and puzzle validation call the resource directly. SupportDefinition supplies the matching model sections to the support scene.
diff --git a/docs/PUZZLE_AUTHORING.md b/docs/PUZZLE_AUTHORING.md
index c3508c9..445c7be 100644
--- a/docs/PUZZLE_AUTHORING.md
+++ b/docs/PUZZLE_AUTHORING.md
@@ -35,7 +35,13 @@ ## Saves and shipping content
 
 ### Scenery pieces
 
-Use **Scenery** in the left construction toolbox for kit decorations, Wall, Floor, Column and Factory machine. All objects share one searchable catalog and placement previews. Walls and columns align visible solid faces and edges; R keeps quarter-turn rotation and Tab cycles nearby alignments. Aim at a column's top to stack another column without changing the active elevation. Free placement uses the construction grid. Click and drag, Shift-click or box-select, rotate, move, duplicate and delete using the normal construction controls. Mixed groups preserve their relative transforms and elevations. Escape cancels a pending edit. The shared contextual toolbar provides rotation, duplication, deletion and elevation for every selection; drag to move.
+The searchable construction toolbox includes **Scenery**, **Architecture**, **Platforms & stairs**, **Factory structures** and **Industrial props**. The curated Building Kit entries include wall variants, square doorways and windows, floor sections, columns, borders, a roof and stairs. Factory Kit entries include crates, catwalks and stairs, support frames, factory doorways, machine props, pipes, a safety cone and a decorative control display. These are fixed scenery during player tests; industrial props do not add working mechanisms.
+
+Aim at an object's visible surface to attach to it. Surface snapping uses the actual collision ray hit and measured occupied volumes; near an edge it also offers the adjacent face. Walls align their ends, floor rims offer flush joins, and top surfaces resolve elevation without changing the active building height. R keeps deliberate quarter-turn rotation; Tab cycles available attachments. Track connectors retain compatible full-3D alignment and connector cycling. When there is no attachment target, placement uses the horizontal grid and active elevation; toggling snapping off provides free grid placement.
+
+Blue previews indicate grid placement, green indicates attachment, and red indicates a blocked placement. A small dot marks the attachment target. Preview and placement use the same overlap checks. Face contact is allowed; penetrating solids is rejected. Doorway/window volumes preserve their openings. Sloping and curved faces do not automatically provide rectangular face attachments; use grid placement or a suitable flat surface. Machinery placement volumes approximate its mesh, while physical collision uses the imported triangles. The control display is decorative and pickable but does not block marbles.
+
+Click and drag, Shift-click or box-select, rotate, duplicate and delete with the shared construction controls. Dragging preserves the grabbed surface height and cursor offset; free movement retains the original elevation. Mixed groups retain their internal transforms and elevation differences, exclude themselves as snap targets, and commit as one undoable edit. Escape cancels a pending edit. Reusable scene composites use these same operations; their internal hierarchy is not edited.
 
 Scenery and track edits share one undo/redo history, including atomic group operations. Columns can intersect walls to form corner posts; their registered definition permits this joint and snapping can centre the column on it. Other scenery and track overlaps remain rejected before committing. Selecting an object does not invalidate its proof. **Save draft** preserves both collections; reopening reconstructs the same objects and native collision. During Test as Player and Campaign, scenery is fixed, excluded from inventory and unavailable to player selection or shortcuts.
 
diff --git a/docs/UI_DESIGN.md b/docs/UI_DESIGN.md
index b576b84..d24fa3c 100644
--- a/docs/UI_DESIGN.md
+++ b/docs/UI_DESIGN.md
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@ ## Layouts and workflows
 
 - Main menu: unboxed left navigation around the animated real-3D showcase, with Campaign emphasized, Sandbox, saves, examples, Puzzle Editor, settings and a separated Quit action.
 - Build: separate content-sized file/history and test/menu islands. A bottom-left library uses recognizable model thumbnails, search and categories for larger catalogs, and a compact one-column layout for single-piece inventories. Bottom contextual strips expose placement helpers and selection actions. Drag selected pieces directly. The right inspector appears only for configurable single pieces; group actions live in the selection strip. Temporary status and Introduction hints occupy bounded contextual cards.
+- Bounded numeric piece settings use the shared `setting_slider.tscn`: live value and units above the slider, minimum and maximum below. Dragging updates the displayed number and commits one configuration/history change on release; arrow keys retain the setting's step size. Authoring limits and inventory counts remain numeric fields.
 - Simulation: hide the library and inspector, emphasize test state and pause/restart/return controls. Preserve camera navigation. No world editing.
 - Track browser: one modal list with load, rename and confirmed deletion. Empty state explains how to create a save. Examples use the same browser.
 - Settings: audio levels, display/graphics options, camera controls, rebindable keyboard shortcuts, and restore defaults. Saved native controls in a scrollable, tabbed modal with predictable focus and Escape behavior.
